### WRITE — send an email (draft → review → send)
Default for outbound: **draft first**, show {{USER_NAME}}, then send on approval.
```bash
# Create a draft, NOT send
{{MINERU_HOME}}/bin/gog-firewall gmail drafts create \
    --to "recipient@example.com" \
    --subject "Subject line" \
    --body-file /tmp/draft-body.txt

# After {{USER_NAME}} approves, send the draft by id
{{MINERU_HOME}}/bin/gog-firewall gmail drafts send <draftId>
```

Direct send (only when explicitly authorized — e.g. self-test, scripted notify):

**Test / smoke-test sends: ALWAYS send to {{USER_NAME}}'s own address (`{{USER_PRIMARY_EMAIL}}`) with a clearly-labeled `{{PERSONA_NAME_LOWER}} test` subject.** Never send test mail to a dummy/placeholder address (e.g. `x@y.com`) — it bounces (Address-not-found) and clutters the inbox with delivery-failure notices, and it reads as alarming. A self-send is delivered, clean, and obviously a test. (Mirrors the iMessage "always self-text when testing" rule.)
```bash
{{MINERU_HOME}}/bin/gog-firewall gmail send \
    --to "{{USER_PRIMARY_EMAIL}}" \
    --subject "{{PERSONA_NAME_LOWER}} test" \
    --body "hello"
```

### WRITE — reply within a thread
`--reply-to-message-id` and `--thread-id` are **mutually exclusive** — the CLI errors if you pass both. Prefer `--reply-to-message-id <gmail msg id>`: it threads the reply AND sets `In-Reply-To`/`References`. Read the `<gmail msg id>` from `gmail thread get <threadId> --plain` (the last `=== Message N/M: <msgId> ===` block). Add `--reply-all` to auto-populate recipients from the original.
```bash
{{MINERU_HOME}}/bin/gog-firewall gmail send \
    --to "them@example.com" \
    --reply-to-message-id <messageId> \
    --subject "Re: ..." \
    --body-file /tmp/reply-body.txt
```

